this is what's left of the once bustling  village of solhan  a group of armed men attacked on friday  night  shooting people and burning down their  homes in what's been described  as a massacre burkina faso's prime  minister  traveled to the remote village near the  border with niger to see what happened  this is the first time our country has  experienced an attack with such massive  destruction to the people of our country  and from this point of view the  president wanted us to spread the  message of hope  we have been fighting against terrorism  for some time we have won some victories  but each time there are new challenges  that arise  the situation in salon is a new  challenge for us  dabire also visited a hospital where  dozens of the injured were taken  several shared stories of the horror  they witnessed  there was so much damage they killed so  many people without even separating the  woman and children  they killed they burned all the goods  they saw  they ransacked the prime minister says  the attack will not go unpunished and  that extra forces have been deployed to  the region  he was joined by defence minister sharif  momenta  who last month promised to step up the  fight against armed groups  but burkina faso's military is seen as  ill-equipped and under-trained  and is facing an increasing number of  attacks as well as ethnic and religious  tension  no one's claimed responsibility for  friday's violence  but analysts believe it was likely  carried out by a group linked to  al-qaeda  particularly  since the arrival of terrorism 2015 this  is the first time that there have been  so many deaths in a single operation  and above all so many civilian deaths  this is why the burkinabe population is  very sad and questions about the ability  of the country  and the defense and security system to  stem the terrorist insurgency are  growing the prime minister says seven  thousand families were displaced by the  latest fighting  adding to the more than one million  who've been forced from their homes in  the past two years  he says the community must work with the  government to ensure  young people aren't recruited by armed  groups  our youth need to stay in the country to  work towards economic and social  development  it's not good to let these young people  leave they're recruited by terrorist  groups and return to villages to bring  terror and sadness to their own families  burkina faso was once seen as a stable  country in the turbulent sahel region  but that reputation has now been  shattered alexi o'brien al jazeera
 

